## Global diameter-direction comparison

The new route replaces absolute positional lattice transport with relative
short-chord arithmetic. For an additive grid with unit increments in two narrow
direction windows, a short physical chord has bounded integer index difference;
its direction approaches one of finitely many lattice-vector directions even
when accumulated positional error stays bounded away from zero.

```text
uniformly conditioned additive charts on the actual loaded endpoints
  -> relative short-chord directions concentrate in finite normal intervals
  -> convex normal cones plus diameter-direction separation give h=o(D)
  -> contradict canonical h>=ceil(D)
```

This attacks a global diameter count rather than constructing a finite packet.
The new proof must establish its arithmetic conclusion without using absolute
position error. The remaining source bridge must cover actual loaded endpoints,
including endpoints near exceptional faces, small boundary grains, and the
macroscopic-container branch. Bulk phase coherence alone is not that bridge.

The relative-chord proof is recorded in
`docs/reports/target_c_relative_chord_diameter_direction_exclusion_2026-10-07.md`.
It yields a finite diameter-edge count and a growing-chart exclusion under
J=o(D), total perimeter O(D), and sum of angular error widths o(1). It allows
accumulated positional errors that do not tend to zero. The exact bent-grid
checker passed 3,640 boundary pairs, including 624 short chords, at M=8,12,16.
No full-source chart coverage is established and no lifecycle is promoted.

